@page "/exgrid/clipboard"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid

<DocsPage Product="ExGrid" Title="Clipboard"
          Lead="Copy and paste behave as a spreadsheet user expects, with Excel on the other end. Where the result would be quietly wrong, the grid refuses instead: a copy is never truncated, and a paste never writes outside the selection.">

    <DocsSection Title="Copy">
        <p>
            Select cells and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>; the Context Menu (right-click, or
            <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F10</kbd>) also offers <em>Copy with headers</em>. Nothing needs wiring.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Two formats go on the clipboard.</strong> The text each cell shows, its <code>format</code>
                applied, as plain text; and the raw values as an HTML table, so Excel receives every digit of an
                amount the grid displays rounded. A cell showing <code>####</code> copies its value, never the hashes.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Rows off screen are copied too.</strong> A selection larger than the rows the grid has painted
                asks the source for the rest. When you push rows yourself, answer <code>OnCopyRowsNeeded</code>;
                without it, such a copy is refused, not cut short.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Several ranges copy as one block</strong> when they line up — the same rows, or the same
                columns — as in Excel.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Your application can answer a copy itself.</strong> Set <code>CopyAnswer</code> and, once the
                grid's own rules have approved a copy, it is handed the ranges being copied
                (<code>GridCopyRequest</code>, with the Row Sequence Version their positions are read under) and the
                grid writes exactly what it returns: <code>GridCopyAnswer.Write(text, html)</code>, or
                <code>GridCopyAnswer.Refuse(sentence)</code>, which leaves the clipboard as it was, announces your
                sentence and raises <code>OnCopyRefused</code> as <code>RefusedByConsumer</code>. It is asked
                synchronously, in the same process, so it answers from data you hold — ExSheet answers its copies
                this way, from the values its formulas give. <code>CopyAnswerAsync</code> is the same answer for data
                you must ask for first, such as a report a server computes, which is how ExPivot copies: the copy
                then always takes the clipboard's asynchronous route, even when its cells are on screen. Note the
                version of your data before you await, and answer from that version or refuse — the grid never fills
                a gap with what it happens to hold now. When both are set, <code>CopyAnswer</code> is the one asked.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>What you copied stays outlined with dashes</strong> while the clipboard still holds it, as
                Excel's moving border does, but standing still. It goes when anything else is copied — in another
                application, another grid or a text field — when you press <kbd>Esc</kbd> or start an edit, and
                when a copied cell comes to show another value. A paste leaves it. A browser that cannot tell the
                page the clipboard changed (Chrome and Edge before 143) shows no outline at all.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Why a copy refuses rather than truncates">
        <p>
            The everyday use of a blotter is to select rows, paste them into Excel and total them there. If a copy
            quietly kept only the first thousand rows, the total would be too small and nothing on screen would say
            so. So past <code>CopyCellCap</code> — a million cells by default — the grid copies <strong>nothing</strong>,
            leaves the clipboard as it was, and raises <code>OnCopyRefused</code> with the reason:
            <code>TooLarge</code>, <code>MisalignedShape</code>, <code>EmptySelection</code>, and a few more. The
            wording is yours. A selection of exactly the cap still copies.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Paste">
        <p>
            Paste into editable columns raises one <code>OnPaste</code> for the whole block: the target ranges and a
            value for every target cell. As with an edit, the grid writes nothing — your application applies it by
            replacing rows. Call <code>Refuse()</code> on the intent to decline it, and the selection stays put.
            Only two of Excel's four shape rules are kept:
        </p>
        <MudSimpleTable Dense="true" Outlined="true" Class="docs-api-table">
            <thead><tr><th style="width: 40%">Copied → selected</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>One cell → a range</td><td>The value fills every selected cell, across disjoint ranges too.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>2 rows → 6 rows</td><td>The block repeats to fill the selection: a target of M×N accepts a block of m×n when m divides M and n divides N.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>A block → one cell</td><td><strong>Refused</strong> (<code>SingleCellTarget</code>). Excel would spill past the selection, into rows that may not even be on screen. Select a range of the block's shape.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>3 rows → 5 rows</td><td><strong>Refused</strong> (<code>ShapeMismatch</code>), as in Excel.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </MudSimpleTable>
        <p>
            A selection that covers a column which is not editable is refused first, as
            <code>TargetNotEditable</code>, since no reselection of the same shape would help. A single value pasted
            as plain text — not as a table — over a range lands in its top-left cell only. Numbers copied from Excel
            arrive at full precision, and <code>Origins</code> tells you whether a field is invariant-culture text
            from Excel's own data or text as it was shown.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Copy out, paste in" Builtin="typeof(ClipboardGrid)" Mud="typeof(ClipboardGridMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/Position.cs"])">
            This grid's cap is set to 2,000 cells so the refusal is easy to see: press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd>,
            then <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>. Then select a few rows and copy again. Quantity and Price take a paste;
            the other columns refuse it.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="API">
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(ExGrid.Clipboard.GridPasteIntent)" />
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(ExGrid.Clipboard.PastePlan)" />
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
